How to build the sheet:
- Group lines into topics in a sensible study order (usually course order). Topic titles are short ("Integration by parts", "Series tests").
- Each line is one thing to recall, written densely as a student would on a sheet: start with the name in bold, then the formula or statement, e.g. "**Ratio test**: $L=\lim_{n\to\infty}\left|\frac{a_{n+1}}{a_n}\right|$; $L<1$ converges abs., $L>1$ diverges, $L=1$ inconclusive".
- Every line lists each id of the items it covers in "items" (["r3", "r4"], not ranges). You may merge items that say the same thing (the same formula in two lectures) into one line, condense wording and drop redundant words. Never add a formula, condition or fact that is not in the items you list.
- Keep formulas exactly as the items give them, in the course's notation, with LaTeX between single dollar signs.
- Choose what to keep by priority: p1 items first, then p2; use p3 only if room remains. Leave out items of kinds not listed above.
- Prefer many short lines over a few long ones; don't repeat the topic title inside each line.

